    "items": [
      {
        "id": "lem-cg-similar-euclidean-simplices-from-shared-facet-normal-gram",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Euclidean simplices with the same facet-normal Gram matrix are similar facet to facet",
        "deps": [
          "def-geometric-simplex-spanned-by-affinely-independent-vertices",
          "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "def-affine-subspace-of-a-vector-space",
          "def-linear-isometry-and-orthogonal-or-unitary-operator",
          "def-isometry-and-metric-embedding",
          "def-linear-basis",
          "def-linear-independence",
          "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map",
          "def-linear-subspace",
          "def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map",
          "def-linear-map",
          "thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-extreme-point-and-face",
          "def-orthogonal-complement",
          "thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition",
          "lem-kernel-basis-extension-gives-image-basis",
          "thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace",
          "def-dimension"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $I$ be a finite set with $|I|=n+1$, let $E$ and $E'$ be Euclidean affine spaces of dimension $n$ with direction spaces $V$ and $V'$ ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-affine-subspace-of-a-vector-space]], [[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]), and let $$\\sigma=\\{\\varphi\\in E:\\langle u_i,\\varphi\\rangle\\ge c_i\\ \\text{for all }i\\in I\\},\\qquad \\sigma'=\\{\\varphi'\\in E':\\langle u_i',\\varphi'\\rangle\\ge c_i'\\ \\text{for all }i\\in I\\}$$ be nonempty bounded intersections of $|I|$ closed half-spaces with unit normals $u_i\\in V$, $u_i'\\in V'$ and offsets $c_i,c_i'\\in\\mathbb R$, each half-space defining a facet of the corresponding set (so each of $\\sigma,\\sigma'$ has exactly $|I|=n+1$ facets, indexed by $I$, and is an $n$-simplex). Suppose $\\langle u_i,u_j\\rangle=\\langle u_i',u_j'\\rangle$ for all $i,j\\in I$. Then there are a linear isometry $T:V\\to V'$, a point $y_0\\in E'$ and a number $t>0$ such that $y_0+t\\,T(\\sigma)=\\sigma'$, and $T$ carries the facet of $\\sigma$ with normal $u_i$ onto the facet of $\\sigma'$ with normal $u_i'$ for every $i\\in I$. In particular the groups generated by the reflections of $E$ in the facets of $\\sigma$ and of $E'$ in the facets of $\\sigma'$ are conjugate by the similarity $y\\mapsto y_0+t\\,Ty$, hence isomorphic as reflection groups acting on Euclidean spaces.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "M. W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (first-edition author manuscript, Princeton University Press, 2008; 600 PDF pages)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 6.8, Lemma 6.8.5 with proof (the unit normal vectors determine a Euclidean simplex up to translation and homothety) and Lemma 6.8.6 (the coefficients of the relation $c_0u_0+\\dots+c_nu_n=0$ are all nonzero and of one sign), printed pp. 99-100"
            }
          ],
          "scraped": []
        },
        "strategy": "**(i) The normals span.** Since $\\sigma$ is bounded, the only vector $v\\in V$ with $\\langle u_i,v\\rangle\\ge0$ for all $i$ is $v=0$; equivalently the $u_i$ positively span $V$, so they span $V$ and every relation among them is unique up to scalar. **(ii) The relation.** Fix a nonzero $p\\in\\mathbb R^I$ with $\\sum_ip_iu_i=0$; the space of such $p$ is one-dimensional because the $u_i$ span an $n$-dimensional space. Using a vertex $v_i$ of $\\sigma$ (opposite the facet $i$), the identity $0=\\sum_jp_j\\langle u_j,v_i\\rangle=p_i\\langle u_i,v_i\\rangle+(R-p_ic_i)$ with $R:=\\sum_jp_jc_j$ and the strict inequality $\\langle u_i,v_i\\rangle>c_i$ force $R/p_i<0$; hence $p_i\\ne0$ and all $p_i$ have the sign of $-R$. Rescale so that $p_i>0$ and $R<0$. **(iii) $T$.** The assignment $u_i\\mapsto u_i'$ preserves inner products and is well defined on the span (both tuples have the same Gram matrix and span their spaces); it extends uniquely to a linear isometry $T:V\\to V'$ with $Tu_i=u_i'$. **(iv) The offsets.** Choosing an interior point of $\\sigma$ gives $0=\\sum_ip_i\\langle u_i,\\varphi\\rangle>\\sum_ip_ic_i=R$, so $R<0$; the same argument for $\\sigma'$ gives $R':=\\sum_ip_ic_i'<0$; put $t:=R'/R>0$. **(v) Solving for the translation.** The vector $\\Delta:=(c_i'-tc_i)_{i\\in I}$ satisfies $\\sum_ip_i\\Delta_i=R'-tR=0$, hence $\\Delta$ is orthogonal to $p$; the image of the linear map $y\\mapsto(\\langle u_i',y\\rangle)_{i\\in I}$ is exactly $p^\\perp$ (it is $n$-dimensional and contained in $p^\\perp$, which is $n$-dimensional), so there is $y_0\\in E'$ with $\\langle u_i',y_0\\rangle=c_i'-tc_i$ for all $i$. **(vi) Conclusion.** For $\\varphi\\in E$: $\\varphi\\in\\sigma\\iff\\langle u_i,\\varphi\\rangle\\ge c_i\\ \\forall i\\iff t\\langle u_i,\\varphi\\rangle+c_i'\\ge c_i'+tc_i\\ \\forall i\\iff\\langle u_i',y_0+tT\\varphi\\rangle\\ge c_i'\\ \\forall i$; so $y_0+tT\\sigma=\\sigma'$ and the facet $i$ is carried to the facet $i$. Reflections in corresponding facets are conjugate by the similarity ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). No Choice is used.",
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "def-cg-irreducible-affine-coxeter-type",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Irreducible affine Coxeter type: the corank-one form, the radical quotient, and the affine slice",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type",
          "def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals",
          "def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form",
          "def-quotient-vector-space-and-canonical-projection",
          "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional",
          "def-affine-subspace-of-a-vector-space",
          "def-linear-subspace",
          "def-linear-basis",
          "thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual",
          "def-dimension"
        ],
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions",
          "thm-cg-affine-gram-classification-and-euclidean-realization"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $S$ be finite, let $m$ be a Coxeter matrix on $S$, let $W$ be the presented\nCoxeter group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), let $\\Gamma$ be\nits Coxeter diagram ([[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]]), and\nlet $V=\\mathbb R^S$ carry the real Coxeter form $B$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]):\n$B(e_s,e_s)=1$, $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite labels, and\n$B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$. Put\n$\\operatorname{rad}(B):=\\{v\\in V:B(v,w)=0\\text{ for every }w\\in V\\}$\n([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]).\n\n**(1) Affine form type.** The system $(W,S)$, and with it $m$, $\\Gamma$ and $B$,\nis of **affine form type** when $\\Gamma$ is connected and $B$ is positive\nsemidefinite (meaning $B(v,v)\\ge0$ for every $v\\in V$) of corank one, that is,\n$\\dim_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{rad}(B)=1$\n([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]],\n[[def-dimension]]). By\n[[lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions]] (1), this is equivalent\nto requiring that $\\Gamma$ be connected, $B$ positive semidefinite, and $B$\nnot positive definite. This is the only notion called *affine* on this page.\nIt is a condition on the Coxeter form, not a synonym for an infinite abstract\nCoxeter group. Disconnected systems are outside this definition; the companion\nexamples page treats reducible forms separately.\n\n**(2) Positive radical vector.** A **positive radical vector** for affine form\ntype is a vector $\\delta=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\delta_se_s\\in\\operatorname{rad}(B)$\nwith $\\delta_s>0$ for every $s\\in S$. For affine form type such a vector\nexists and $\\operatorname{rad}(B)=\\mathbb R\\delta$, by\n[[lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions]] (1); this clause names\nthe object and records the property supplied by that lemma.\n\n**(3) Radical quotient.** Let $U:=V/\\operatorname{rad}(B)$\n([[def-quotient-vector-space-and-canonical-projection]]), and define\n$b(\\bar v,\\bar w):=B(v,w)$. The form $b$ is well-defined, symmetric and\npositive definite. Thus $U$ is a Euclidean vector space\n([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]) of dimension $|S|-1$\n([[def-linear-subspace]], [[def-linear-basis]], [[def-dimension]]).\n\n**(4) Affine slice and walls.** For a positive radical vector $\\delta$, put\n$$E_\\delta:=\\{\\varphi\\in V^*:\\varphi(\\delta)=1\\},$$\nwhere $V^*$ is the algebraic dual ([[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]).\nThis is an affine subspace ([[def-affine-subspace-of-a-vector-space]]) with\ndirection space\n$$K_\\delta:=\\{\\varphi\\in V^*:\\varphi(\\delta)=0\\}=\\operatorname{Ann}(\\operatorname{rad}(B)).$$\nPrecomposition with the quotient projection identifies $K_\\delta$ with $U^*$.\nLet $b^\\flat:U\\to U^*$ be $b^\\flat(u):=b(u,\\cdot)$ ([[thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual]]); it is an isomorphism, as\nverified in the remarks, and the dual form is\n$$b^*(\\alpha,\\beta):=b((b^\\flat)^{-1}\\alpha,(b^\\flat)^{-1}\\beta).$$\nTransporting $b^*$ to the direction space $K_\\delta$ makes $E_\\delta$ a\nEuclidean affine space of dimension $|S|-1$. For $s\\in S$, its **wall** is\n$H_s:=\\{\\varphi\\in E_\\delta:\\varphi(e_s)=0\\}$, its **open alcove** is\n$A:=\\{\\varphi\\in E_\\delta:\\varphi(e_s)>0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\}$,\nand $\\bar A$ denotes the closure of $A$ in $E_\\delta$.\n\n**(5) Abstentions and conventions.** Beyond the constructions and properties\nabove, this definition does not assert that the $H_s$ are affine hyperplanes,\nthat $A$ is nonempty, bounded or a simplex, that $W$ acts on $E_\\delta$ as a\ngroup generated by affine reflections, or that the translates $w\\bar A$ cover\n$E_\\delta$. The phrase *affine form type* refers only to the Coxeter-matrix\ncondition in (1); extended affine Weyl groups and the directed affine Dynkin\ndiagrams used in Lie theory are separate conventions and are not defined here.\nNo choice principle is used in these finite-dimensional constructions.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
