      {
        "id": "lem-cg-affine-slice-simplex-and-wall-reflections",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The affine slice: faithful isometric action, the alcove simplex, and its facet reflections",
        "deps": [
          "def-cg-irreducible-affine-coxeter-type",
          "lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions",
          "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms",
          "thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness",
          "def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes",
          "thm-cg-dual-chamber-intersections-and-point-stabilizers",
          "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "def-generated-subgroup",
          "def-geometric-simplex-spanned-by-affinely-independent-vertices",
          "def-isometry-and-metric-embedding",
          "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "def-metric-topology",
          "lem-locally-convex-closures-and-finite-compact-convex-hulls"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $(W,S,m,V,B,\\rho,\\Gamma)$ be of affine form type, and let $\\delta=\\sum_{s\\in S}\\delta_se_s$ be positive with $\\operatorname{rad}(B)=\\mathbb R\\delta$ ([[def-cg-irreducible-affine-coxeter-type]] (1)-(4), [[lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions]] (1)). Put $U=V/\\operatorname{rad}(B)$ with its quotient Euclidean form $b$, and let $E=E_\\delta$, $H_s$, $A$, and $\\bar A$ be as in [[def-cg-irreducible-affine-coxeter-type]] (3)-(4). Let $\\rho^*(w)\\varphi=\\varphi\\circ\\rho(w)^{-1}$ be the dual action ([[def-cg-dual-chambers-and-reflection-hyperplanes]] (1)); give the direction space $K_\\delta$ of $E$ its dual Euclidean form $b^*$ from (4). For each $s\\in S$ let $\\bar e_s$ be the image of $e_s$ in $U$.\n\n**(1) The action.** Every $\\rho^*(w)$ preserves $E$ and acts there by an affine isometry. It sends $H_s$ to $\\{\\varphi\\in E:\\varphi(\\rho(w)e_s)=0\\}$ and permutes the family of alcove translates $\\{\\rho^*(w)A:w\\in W\\}$. The induced homomorphism $W\\to\\operatorname{Isom}(E)$ is faithful.\n\n**(2) The affine relation.** For every $\\varphi\\in E$, $$\\sum_{s\\in S}\\delta_s\\varphi(e_s)=\\varphi(\\delta)=1.$$\n\n**(3) The alcove is a simplex.** For each $s\\in S$ there is exactly one point $v_s\\in E$ with $v_s(e_t)=0$ for $t\\ne s$, and it satisfies $v_s(e_s)=1/\\delta_s$ and $v_s\\in\\bar A$. The points $(v_s)_{s\\in S}$ are affinely independent and $$\\bar A=\\operatorname{conv}\\{v_s:s\\in S\\}$$ is a Euclidean simplex of dimension $|S|-1$ ([[def-geometric-simplex-spanned-by-affinely-independent-vertices]]). Its facets are $\\bar A\\cap H_s$. The inward unit normal to the facet $\\bar A\\cap H_s$ is $b^\\flat(\\bar e_s)\\in K_\\delta$, equivalently $\\bar e_s$ under the identification $K_\\delta\\cong U$; its Gram matrix is $$b^*(b^\\flat(\\bar e_s),b^\\flat(\\bar e_t))=b(\\bar e_s,\\bar e_t)=B(e_s,e_t).$$ In particular, $A$ is nonempty and $\\bar A$ is compact.\n\n**(4) Facet reflections.** For every $s\\in S$, $\\rho^*(s)|_E$ is the reflection in $H_s$; thus the action on $E$ is generated by the $|S|$ facet reflections of $\\bar A$. Every point of $A$ has trivial stabilizer.\n\n**(5) Alcove intersections.** If $w\\ne u$, then $\\rho^*(w)A\\cap\\rho^*(u)A=\\varnothing$. For every $w\\in W$, $$\\rho^*(w)\\bar A\\cap\\bar A=\\{\\varphi\\in\\bar A:\\varphi(e_s)=0\\text{ for all }s\\in S(w)\\},$$ the closed face of $\\bar A$ indexed by $S(w)$ (allowing the empty face when $S(w)=S$), where $S(w)$ is the support of $w$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)). This intersection has nonempty interior in $E$ only when $w=1$.\n\n**(6) Abstention.** This lemma does not assert that $E=\\bigcup_{w\\in W}\\rho^*(w)\\bar A$, or that the action is properly discontinuous or cocompact. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "M. W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (first-edition author manuscript, Princeton University Press, 2008; 600 PDF pages)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 6.3, Lemma 6.3.10 and proof (printed pp. 80-81; simplex conclusion in the irreducible compact nonobtuse setting); Section 6.8, Lemmas 6.8.5-6.8.7 and Proposition 6.8.8 with proofs (printed pp. 99-101; positive relation, unit normals, and Euclidean simplex construction). These are comparison results; the slice argument is proved directly from its coordinates."
            },
            {
              "title": "M. W. Davis and G. Moussong, Notes on nonpositively curved polyhedra (Turan Workshop lecture notes, 1998/1999; 65 PDF pages)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/notes.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 6.1, Example 6.1.4 (PDF pp. 33-34; the Euclidean simplicial cosine-matrix criterion, used as background only)."
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "The action preserves the radical and hence the affine slice; the induced quotient isometry gives the dual metric action, and pointwise triviality on the slice forces triviality on the full dual. Coordinate evaluations give explicit vertices and barycentric coordinates. Their continuity, proved by Cauchy-Schwarz, identifies the closure with the nonnegative coordinate region; this yields the simplex, its facets, compactness and normals. The reflection formula gives each wall reflection, while the chamber-intersection rule and the reduced-word support theorem give stabilizers, intersections and disjoint interiors. No Choice is used.",
        "dependency_level": 15,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-cg-affine-type-crystallographic-alcove-diagrams",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Crystallographic alcove diagrams: the affine list realized by Weyl types A–G",
        "deps": [
          "def-cg-standard-affine-diagrams",
          "lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions",
          "def-cg-crystallographic-scaling-coroot-and-lattice",
          "lem-cg-integer-pairings-and-allowed-dihedral-labels",
          "thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability",
          "def-cg-affine-root-hyperplane-reflection-and-alcove",
          "lem-cg-highest-root-and-fundamental-alcove",
          "lem-cg-affine-point-stabilizers-and-vertex-residues",
          "thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length",
          "prop-highest-root-exists-and-is-unique-in-an-irreducible-finite-root-system",
          "ex-classical-root-systems-in-euclidean-coordinates",
          "thm-rank-two-root-system-classification",
          "prop-the-weyl-group-is-finite-and-acts-faithfully-on-the-root-system",
          "def-reduced-crystallographic-euclidean-root-system",
          "def-coroot-and-dual-root-system",
          "def-weyl-group-of-a-root-system",
          "def-root-lattice-coroot-lattice-weight-lattice-and-coweight-lattice",
          "thm-simple-roots-form-a-basis-and-every-root-has-one-sign-of-integral-coordinates",
          "def-positive-system-and-base-of-simple-roots",
          "def-height-of-a-root-and-highest-root",
          "def-linear-basis",
          "def-graph-isomorphism-and-complement",
          "def-cartan-matrix-of-a-based-root-system",
          "def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms",
          "def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals",
          "lem-cg-affine-reflection-identities-and-local-finiteness",
          "thm-a-based-root-system-is-determined-up-to-isomorphism-by-its-cartan-matrix"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $\\Phi\\ne\\varnothing$ be an irreducible reduced crystallographic Euclidean root system with positive system and base $\\Delta=\\{\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_n\\}$. Its finite Weyl group and chosen simple-root lengths give a crystallographic scaling of the finite Coxeter form; by [[thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability]] the based finite type is one of $A_n$ ($n\\ge1$), $B_n$ or $C_n$ ($n\\ge2$), $D_n$ ($n\\ge4$), $E_6,E_7,E_8,F_4$, or $G_2$. The root lengths of $\\Phi$ are part of the chosen root system: in particular the two rank-two systems denoted $B_2$ and $C_2$ have the same finite Coxeter diagram but the two dual root-length assignments. Use the standard simple-root numbering: the classical coordinate bases of [[ex-classical-root-systems-in-euclidean-coordinates]], Bourbaki numbering for $E_6,E_7,E_8,F_4$, and $\\alpha_1$ short and $\\alpha_2$ long for $G_2$. Let $\\theta$ be the highest root and let\n$$A_\\Phi:=\\{x\\in E:(x,\\alpha_i)>0\\ (1\\le i\\le n),\\ (x,\\theta)<1\\}$$\nbe the fundamental alcove of [[lem-cg-highest-root-and-fundamental-alcove]]. Put $s_0=r_{\\theta,1}$ and $s_i=r_{\\alpha_i,0}$ for $1\\le i\\le n$, and define $m^\\Phi_{ij}:=\\operatorname{ord}(s_is_j)$, allowing $m^\\Phi_{ij}=\\infty$.\n\n**(1) Highest-root table and affine labels.** The highest roots and the new labels from the affine facet $H_{\\theta,1}$ are as follows; all unlisted $m^\\Phi_{0i}$ equal $2$, and the entries $m^\\Phi_{ij}$ for $i,j\\ge1$ are the finite-type labels.\n\n- $A_n$: $\\theta=\\alpha_1+\\cdots+\\alpha_n$. If $n=1$, $m^\\Phi_{01}=\\infty$. If $n\\ge2$, $m^\\Phi_{01}=m^\\Phi_{0n}=3$.\n- $B_n$: $\\theta=\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_2+\\cdots+2\\alpha_n$. For $n\\ge3$, $m^\\Phi_{02}=3$; for $n=2$, $m^\\Phi_{02}=4$.\n- $C_n$: $\\theta=2\\alpha_1+\\cdots+2\\alpha_{n-1}+\\alpha_n$ and $m^\\Phi_{01}=4$.\n- $D_n$: $\\theta=\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_2+\\cdots+2\\alpha_{n-2}+\\alpha_{n-1}+\\alpha_n$ and $m^\\Phi_{02}=3$.\n- $E_6$: $\\theta=\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_2+2\\alpha_3+3\\alpha_4+2\\alpha_5+\\alpha_6$ and $m^\\Phi_{02}=3$.\n- $E_7$: $\\theta=2\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_2+3\\alpha_3+4\\alpha_4+3\\alpha_5+2\\alpha_6+\\alpha_7$ and $m^\\Phi_{01}=3$.\n- $E_8$: $\\theta=2\\alpha_1+3\\alpha_2+4\\alpha_3+6\\alpha_4+5\\alpha_5+4\\alpha_6+3\\alpha_7+2\\alpha_8$ and $m^\\Phi_{08}=3$.\n- $F_4$: $\\theta=2\\alpha_1+3\\alpha_2+4\\alpha_3+2\\alpha_4$ and $m^\\Phi_{01}=3$.\n- $G_2$: $\\theta=3\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_2$, with $m^\\Phi_{02}=3$, $m^\\Phi_{01}=2$, and $m^\\Phi_{12}=6$.\n\nThus $m^\\Phi$ is the standard affine diagram of the corresponding line of [[def-cg-standard-affine-diagrams]]; for $B_2$ and $C_2$ it is the common path with labels $(4,4)$.\n\n**(2) Facet-normal Gram matrix.** The inward unit normals of $A_\\Phi$ are $u_0=-\\theta/\\|\\theta\\|$ and $u_i=\\alpha_i/\\|\\alpha_i\\|$ for $i\\ge1$. Their Gram matrix is the cosine matrix of $m^\\Phi$:\n$$ (u_i,u_j)=-\\cos(\\pi/m^\\Phi_{ij}), $$\nwhere $\\cos(\\pi/\\infty)=1$. Hence the facet-reflection matrix of $A_\\Phi$ equals the cosine matrix of the displayed affine diagram.\n\n**(3) Semidefinite consequences.** For every standard affine diagram $D$, its cosine matrix $C_D$ is positive semidefinite of corank one and has a kernel vector with every coordinate positive. Every proper principal submatrix of $C_D$ is positive definite; the empty principal submatrix case is vacuous.\n\n**(4) Surjectivity.** Every standard affine diagram occurs in (1): $\\tilde A_1$ is obtained from $A_1$; $\\tilde A_n$ from $A_n$ for $n\\ge2$; $\\tilde B_n$ from $B_n$ for $n\\ge3$; $\\tilde B_2=\\tilde C_2$ from either $B_2$ or $C_2$; and $\\tilde C_n$ from $C_n$ for $n\\ge2$, $\\tilde D_n$ from $D_n$ for $n\\ge4$, and $\\tilde E_6,\\tilde E_7,\\tilde E_8,\\tilde F_4,\\tilde G_2$ from their matching finite types. The aliases $\\tilde D_3=\\tilde A_3$, $\\tilde E_4=\\tilde A_4$, and $\\tilde E_5=\\tilde D_5$ are therefore realized by $A_3,A_4,D_5$, respectively. The convention $\\tilde C_1:=\\tilde A_1$ is covered by $A_1$.\n\n**(5) Affine Weyl group and simplex reflection presentation.** The assignment from the standard generators of the abstract Coxeter group $W(m^\\Phi)$ to $s_0,\\ldots,s_n$ is an isomorphism onto $W_a(\\Phi)$, and\n$$W_a(\\Phi)=Q^\\vee\\rtimes W(\\Phi).$$\nThus each standard affine diagram is the Coxeter diagram of a Euclidean simplex reflection group with fundamental alcove $A_\\Phi$. In rank one the two endpoint hyperplanes are disjoint and their product has infinite order.\n\n**(6) Verification data.** The coefficient vectors in (1) are those of the highest roots in the stated standard numbering. For simply laced types $A,D,E$, the values $2c_i-\\sum_{j\\sim i}c_j$ for $\\theta=\\sum_i c_i\\alpha_i$ give the displayed attachment node (and for $A_1$ the single value is $2$). For $B,C,F,G$, the coordinate root models and the root-length ratios give exactly the normalized pairings recorded in the proof below. The $B_2/C_2$ coincidence is only a coincidence of the finite Coxeter diagram; the two root-length assignments are both included.\n\n**(7) Non-isomorphism.** Apart from the naming conventions $\\tilde C_1=\\tilde A_1$, $\\tilde B_2=\\tilde C_2$, $\\tilde D_3=\\tilde A_3$, $\\tilde E_4=\\tilde A_4$, and $\\tilde E_5=\\tilde D_5$ in [[def-cg-standard-affine-diagrams]], the diagrams in clauses (1)–(6) of that definition are pairwise non-isomorphic as labelled graphs. No twisted affine diagrams or extended affine Weyl group $P^\\vee\\rtimes W$ are asserted here. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
