          "def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $S$ be finite, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented Coxeter group with length $\\ell$, $V=\\mathbb R^S$, the Coxeter form $B$, the canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho$ and the diagram $\\Gamma$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]]).\n\n**(1) Classification.** Assume $\\Gamma$ is connected. Then $B$ is positive semidefinite of corank one ([[def-cg-irreducible-affine-coxeter-type]] (1)) if and only if $\\Gamma$ is isomorphic as a labelled graph to one of the standard affine diagrams $$\\tilde A_1;\\quad \\tilde A_n\\ (n\\ge2);\\quad \\tilde B_n\\ (n\\ge3);\\quad \\tilde C_n\\ (n\\ge2);\\quad \\tilde D_n\\ (n\\ge4);\\quad \\tilde E_6,\\ \\tilde E_7,\\ \\tilde E_8;\\quad \\tilde F_4;\\quad \\tilde G_2$$ ([[def-cg-standard-affine-diagrams]]), with the low-rank coincidences $\\tilde A_1=\\tilde C_1$, $\\tilde B_2=\\tilde C_2$, $\\tilde D_3=\\tilde A_3$, $\\tilde E_4=\\tilde A_4$, $\\tilde E_5=\\tilde D_5$; modulo these the list is duplicate-free, $\\tilde A_1$ is the only diagram with a label $\\infty$, and every other label lies in $\\{3,4,6\\}$. Equivalently: $\\Gamma$ is connected, $B$ is positive semidefinite and $B$ is not positive definite.\n\n**(2) The radical and the affine slice.** For a connected affine $\\Gamma$: $\\operatorname{rad}(B)=\\mathbb R\\delta$ for a positive radical vector $\\delta$ ([[lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions]] (1)); with the radical quotient $U$, the Euclidean form $b$, the affine slice $E=E_\\delta$, the walls $H_s$ and the alcove $A$, the conclusions of [[lem-cg-affine-slice-simplex-and-wall-reflections]] hold: the dual action of $W$ on $E$ is faithful and by affine isometries, $\\bar A=\\operatorname{conv}\\{v_s\\}$ is a Euclidean simplex of dimension $|S|-1$ whose facets are the walls $H_s$, $\\sum_s\\delta_s\\varphi(e_s)=1$ on $E$, the facet normals have Gram matrix $C=(B(e_s,e_t))$, distinct alcove interiors are disjoint and $w\\bar A\\cap\\bar A$ is the closed face of type $S(w)$.\n\n**(3) Realization and matching with the crystallographic alcoves.** If $\\Gamma$ is one of the standard diagrams $D$, let $\\Phi$ be an irreducible finite crystallographic root system of the matching Weyl type in [[lem-cg-affine-type-crystallographic-alcove-diagrams]] (1) (existing by [[thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability]] (2)), with affine Weyl group $W_a(\\Phi)=Q^\\vee\\rtimes W(\\Phi)$, fundamental alcove $A_\\Phi$ and facet reflections $s_0,s_1,\\dots$ ([[def-cg-affine-root-hyperplane-reflection-and-alcove]], [[lem-cg-highest-root-and-fundamental-alcove]], [[lem-cg-affine-alcove-separation-and-facet-types]]). Then the facet-reflection matrix of $A_\\Phi$ equals the Coxeter matrix of $D$ ([[lem-cg-affine-type-crystallographic-alcove-diagrams]] (1)), so $W\\cong W(m)\\cong W_a(\\Phi)$ ([[thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length]] (2)); $W_a(\\Phi)$ acts on its Euclidean space properly discontinuously and cocompactly, $\\overline{A_\\Phi}$ is a strict fundamental domain, and $W_a(\\Phi)$ acts simply transitively on the alcoves, with $\\ell(g)$ the number of walls separating $A_\\Phi$ from $gA_\\Phi$ (clauses (1)-(3) of that theorem). Moreover the inward unit normals of the facets of $A_\\Phi$ have the same Gram matrix $C$ as the facet normals of the slice simplex $\\bar A$ of (2), with facets matched by the labelling; by [[lem-cg-similar-euclidean-simplices-from-shared-facet-normal-gram]] the two simplices are similar with facets matched, so the similarity intertwines the two reflection group actions. Consequently, for the slice realization of (2): the alcoves $w\\bar A$ ($w\\in W$) tile $E$, $W$ acts properly discontinuously and cocompactly on $E$ with $\\bar A$ a strict fundamental domain, $W$ acts simply transitively on the alcoves, and $\\ell(w)$ is the number of walls separating $\\bar A$ from $w\\bar A$.\n\n**(4) Consequences and conventions.** For connected $\\Gamma$: $W$ is finite if and only if $B$ is positive definite ([[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]] (1)); if $\\Gamma$ is affine then $W$ is infinite and every proper standard parabolic $W_T$ $(T\\subsetneq S)$ is finite ([[lem-cg-positive-radical-and-affine-gram-exclusions]] (2)), and conversely an infinite connected Coxeter system with all proper parabolics finite need not be affine: its form need not be positive semidefinite (the companion page gives an indefinite example). Equivalently, a connected system is of affine form type if and only if it has a faithful Euclidean simplex reflection realization with a bounded fundamental Coxeter chamber, whose interior dihedral angles are $\\pi/m_{st}$ and whose facet-reflection matrix is its own Coxeter matrix, the realization being the one constructed in (2)-(3). The facet-generated group of this realization is $Q^\\vee\\rtimes W(\\Phi)$; the extended group $P^\\vee\\rtimes W(\\Phi)$ strictly contains it when $P^\\vee\\ne Q^\\vee$, and coincides with it when the two lattices are equal ([[thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length]] (4)); no assertion is made about twisted Lie-theoretic diagrams. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
