        "statement": "Compare the standard affine diagrams $\\tilde B_n$ and $\\tilde C_n$ ([[def-cg-standard-affine-diagrams]] (2),(3)) and the crystallographic scalings that produce them:\n\n**(i) The coincidence at $n=2$.** $\\tilde B_2=\\tilde C_2$ is the three-vertex path with edge labels $(4,4)$: the $n=2$ member is defined by the coincidence convention, and both its edges carry label $4$. Both arise from the same finite type: $B_2$ and $C_2$ are the same Coxeter system ([[thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability]] (1) and the finite coincidence $B_2=C_2=I_2(4)$ of [[thm-cg-finite-coxeter-classification-including-h-and-dihedral]] (4)), and the $B_n$- and $C_n$-scalings with $n=2$ are exchanged by duality ([[thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability]] (4)).\n\n**(ii) The difference for $n\\ge3$.** $\\tilde B_n$ has a vertex of degree $3$ (the vertex $v_2$ carrying the extra $v_0$) and exactly one edge labelled $4$, namely $\\{v_{n-1},v_n\\}$; $\\tilde C_n$ is a path and has exactly two edges labelled $4$, at its two ends. Hence for $n\\ge3$ the two labelled graphs are not isomorphic ([[def-graph-isomorphism-and-complement]]): the degree sequences disagree.\n\n**(iii) Both are affine.** By [[lem-cg-affine-type-crystallographic-alcove-diagrams]] (3), the cosine matrices of $\\tilde B_n$ and $\\tilde C_n$ are positive semidefinite of corank one with positive kernel vectors; concretely, for $\\tilde C_n$ the vector $(1,\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2,\\dots,\\sqrt2,1)$ (first and last entry $1$) lies in the kernel, and for $\\tilde B_n$ with $n\\ge3$ the vector $(1,1,2,2,\\dots,2,\\sqrt2)$ (entries ordered $v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3,\\dots,v_n$, i.e. $x_0=x_1=1$, $x_2=\\dots=x_{n-1}=2$, $x_n=\\sqrt2$) lies in the kernel; in both cases the entries are positive.\n\n**(iv) Why the Coxeter diagram alone does not decide the lattice.** $B_n$ and $C_n$ have the same finite Coxeter diagram but their two crystallographic scalings are dual ([[thm-cg-crystallographic-finite-type-and-lattice-stability]] (4), [[def-cg-crystallographic-scaling-coroot-and-lattice]]); the fundamental alcoves are bounded $n$-simplices (triangles when $n=2$), but the affine Weyl groups $W_a(B_n)=Q^\\vee\\rtimes W$ and $W_a(C_n)={Q^\\vee}'\\rtimes W$ ([[lem-cg-affine-reflection-identities-and-local-finiteness]] (4)) realize the two different Coxeter diagrams $\\tilde B_n$ and $\\tilde C_n$ for $n\\ge3$ ([[lem-cg-affine-type-crystallographic-alcove-diagrams]] (1),(5), [[thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length]] (2)), and for $n\\ge3$ they are not isomorphic even as abstract groups: $W_a(B_n)$ has a maximal finite subgroup of order $2^{n-1}n!$, whereas $W_a(C_n)$ has none, as proved below. Thus the Coxeter diagram of the finite system does not determine which lattice acts.\n\n**(v) Convention warning.** The labels $4$ here are orders of facet-reflection products, i.e. Coxeter labels ($\\cos(\\pi/4)={\\sqrt2}/{2}$), not Lie-theoretic bond multiplicities; the extended affine Weyl group $P^\\vee\\rtimes W$ is a further, different object ([[thm-cg-affine-alcove-transitivity-presentation-and-length]] (4)).",
